 ],
 "title": "Learn Programming with OCaml: Algorithms and Data Structures",
 "byline": "Sylvain Conchon and Jean-Christophe Filli\u00e2tre \u00b7 English translation by Urmila Nair, OCaml Software Foundation, February 2025",
 "note": "PDF page = printed page + 10. Exercise sections are not broken out. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A two-part book that teaches programming in OCaml and then classic algorithms and data structures. Part I introduces the language through fourteen small, complete programs (leap years, Monte Carlo, the Mandelbrot set, the sieve, file copying, a Breakout clone, Logo turtle, music, quadtrees, N-queens) and then explains type checking, the runtime model and complexity. Part II implements and analyzes data structures and algorithms in a functional-first style: resizable and persistent arrays, bit vectors and ropes; search trees, AVL, hash tables, tries and Patricia trees; imperative and persistent queues and heaps; graph representations; union\u2013find; zippers; arithmetic algorithms; memoization and hash-consing; sorting; and graph algorithms."
}